 "last_rtl_message": {
  "stage": "rtl-design-creation",
  "visit": 4,
  "text": "I changed `rtl/p2_mem_test_core.sv` so host accesses made while the power-on counter still holds `rst` high are no longer swallowed. I couldn't run the independent spec bench itself, so the fix is untested against it. The design's own bench passes (3 passed), `lint` and `standards` pass at rtl scope, and `resets` was not run.\n\n**Cause.** The spec bench drives the top's internal `rst` and `h_*` nets. The top's `por_gen` keeps `rst` high for 16 clocks and overrides the bench's deposit, so the core was still in reset when the bench started its accesses. The old core cleared `rd_pend` and its registers on every clock of `rst`, so `h_rdata` was never loaded and stayed X. Writes made in that window, such as the AUTOINC write to MEM_CTRL, would also have been wiped.\n\n**Fix.**\n- The core now applies its reset once, on the rising edge of `rst`, instead of on every clock `rst` is high. The same pulse still steps the RAM epoch that restores the RAM contents.\n- `h_rdata` gets a power-up value of 0.\n\nI first tried removing the reset from the core entirely. That broke 5 of the design's 11 bench tests, which rely on a reset between tests, so I reverted to the rising-edge version above.\n\nOne risk: the pulse should land at clock 7 in the spec bench, before its first access at clock 9. That is from reading the code, not from a simulation of the spec bench.\n\nI did not run the full `check` with `[\"all\"]`.\n\nDONE"
 },
